# Angular Two-Way Binding

## 1. Quick recap of data binding

Angular data binding connects the component class with its HTML template.

```text
TypeScript class → stores data and logic
HTML template    → displays data and receives user input
```

Angular provides four main types of data binding:

| Binding | Syntax | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Interpolation | `{{ value }}` | Displays data as text |
| Property binding | `[property]="value"` | Sends data to an element property |
| Event binding | `(event)="method()"` | Responds to user actions |
| Two-way binding | `[(ngModel)]="value"` | Keeps data and input synchronized |

Two-way binding combines property binding and event binding.

---

# 2. What is two-way binding?

Two-way binding allows data to move in both directions:

```text
Component → HTML element
HTML element → Component
```

For example:

1. The component provides an initial username.
2. The input displays that username.
3. The user changes the input.
4. The component property updates automatically.
5. Any other template using that property displays the new value.

```text
Component value
      ↓
Input field
      ↓
User changes the input
      ↓
Component value updates
      ↓
The page displays the new value
```

Two-way binding is mainly used with:

- Text inputs
- Dropdowns
- Checkboxes
- Other form controls

---

# 3. The `ngModel` directive

Angular provides the `ngModel` directive for two-way binding.

```html
<input [(ngModel)]="username">
```

This means:

> Keep the input field and the `username` property synchronized.

The syntax is called:

```text
Banana in a box
```

Why?

```text
[(ngModel)]
```

The round brackets look like a banana inside the square brackets.

---

# 4. How two-way binding works internally

This:

```html
<input [(ngModel)]="username">
```

combines:

```html
<input
  [ngModel]="username"
  (ngModelChange)="username = $event">
```

The two parts have different responsibilities:

```text
[ngModel]                 → sends username to the input
(ngModelChange)           → sends the changed value back
```

Two-way binding simply combines both operations into one shorter syntax.

---

# 5. Enable `ngModel` with `FormsModule`

`ngModel` belongs to Angular's `FormsModule`.

## Standalone component setup

Open `data-binding.ts`:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-data-binding',
  imports: [FormsModule],
  templateUrl: './data-binding.html',
  styleUrl: './data-binding.css'
})
export class DataBinding {
  username = 'Abhi';
}
```

Two things are important:

```typescript
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
```

This imports `FormsModule`.

```typescript
imports: [FormsModule]
```

This makes the `ngModel` directive available in the component template.

---

# 6. Two-way binding with a text input

Open `data-binding.html`:

```html
<input
  type="text"
  [(ngModel)]="username"
  placeholder="Enter your name">

<p>Hello, {{ username }}!</p>
```

## What happens?

When the page loads:

```typescript
username = 'Abhi';
```

The input displays:

```text
Abhi
```

When the user types a new name:

```text
The input value changes
        ↓
username updates
        ↓
{{ username }} displays the new value
```

No separate event method is required.

---

# 7. Two-way binding with a dropdown

## Component class

```typescript
course = 'Angular';
```

## Template

```html
<select [(ngModel)]="course">
  <option value="Angular">Angular</option>
  <option value="TypeScript">TypeScript</option>
  <option value="HTML">HTML</option>
</select>

<p>Selected course: {{ course }}</p>
```

If the user selects `TypeScript`, the `course` property automatically becomes:

```typescript
course = 'TypeScript';
```

The paragraph then updates automatically.

---

# 8. Two-way binding with a checkbox

## Component class

```typescript
isSubscribed = false;
```

## Template

```html
<input
  type="checkbox"
  [(ngModel)]="isSubscribed">

<p>Subscribed: {{ isSubscribed }}</p>
```

When the checkbox is selected:

```text
isSubscribed = true
```

When it is unselected:

```text
isSubscribed = false
```

A checkbox usually works with a boolean value.

---

# 9. Complete example

## `data-binding.ts`

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-data-binding',
  imports: [FormsModule],
  templateUrl: './data-binding.html',
  styleUrl: './data-binding.css'
})
export class DataBinding {
  username = 'Abhi';
  course = 'Angular';
  isSubscribed = false;
}
```

## `data-binding.html`

```html
<h2>Student Preferences</h2>

<label>
  Name:
  <input
    type="text"
    [(ngModel)]="username">
</label>

<br><br>

<label>
  Course:
  <select [(ngModel)]="course">
    <option value="Angular">Angular</option>
    <option value="TypeScript">TypeScript</option>
    <option value="HTML">HTML</option>
  </select>
</label>

<br><br>

<label>
  <input
    type="checkbox"
    [(ngModel)]="isSubscribed">

  Subscribe to updates
</label>

<hr>

<h3>Live Preview</h3>

<p>Name: {{ username }}</p>
<p>Course: {{ course }}</p>
<p>Subscribed: {{ isSubscribed }}</p>
```

This example uses:

```text
[(ngModel)] → keeps form controls and component data synchronized
{{ }}       → displays the current values
```

