# 10. Common mistakes

## Mistake 1: Forgetting `FormsModule`

Incorrect:

```typescript
@Component({
  imports: [],
  // ...
})
```

Correct:

```typescript
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  imports: [FormsModule],
  // ...
})
```

Without `FormsModule`, Angular may show:

```text
Can't bind to 'ngModel' since it isn't a known property of 'input'.
```

---

## Mistake 2: Writing the brackets in the wrong order

Incorrect:

```html
<input ([ngModel])="username">
```

Correct:

```html
<input [(ngModel)]="username">
```

The correct order is:

```text
[ ( ngModel ) ]
```

The round brackets are inside the square brackets.

---

## Mistake 3: Binding to a property that does not exist

Template:

```html
<input [(ngModel)]="username">
```

The component must contain:

```typescript
username = '';
```

If `username` is not declared, Angular will report a template error.

---

## Mistake 4: Using the wrong data type

For a checkbox, use a boolean:

```typescript
isSubscribed = false;
```

For a text input, use a string:

```typescript
username = '';
```

For a dropdown, use a string or number that matches the option values.

---

## Mistake 5: Forgetting `name` inside a form

If `ngModel` is used inside a `<form>`, give each control a `name`:

```html
<form>
  <input
    name="username"
    [(ngModel)]="username">

  <select
    name="course"
    [(ngModel)]="course">
  </select>
</form>
```

---

# 11. Two-way binding with a registration form

## Component class

```typescript
export class Registration {
  name = '';
  email = '';
  course = 'Angular';
  agreed = false;

  onSubmit() {
    console.log({
      name: this.name,
      email: this.email,
      course: this.course,
      agreed: this.agreed
    });
  }
}
```

## Template

```html
<h2>Registration Form</h2>

<form>
  <label>
    Name:
    <input
      name="name"
      [(ngModel)]="name">
  </label>

  <br><br>

  <label>
    Email:
    <input
      name="email"
      type="email"
      [(ngModel)]="email">
  </label>

  <br><br>

  <label>
    Course:
    <select
      name="course"
      [(ngModel)]="course">
      <option value="Angular">Angular</option>
      <option value="TypeScript">TypeScript</option>
    </select>
  </label>

  <br><br>

  <label>
    <input
      name="agreed"
      type="checkbox"
      [(ngModel)]="agreed">

    I agree to the terms
  </label>

  <br><br>

  <button
    type="button"
    [disabled]="!agreed"
    (click)="onSubmit()">
    Submit
  </button>
</form>

<hr>

<h3>Live Preview</h3>

<p>Name: {{ name }}</p>
<p>Email: {{ email }}</p>
<p>Course: {{ course }}</p>
<p>Agreed: {{ agreed }}</p>
```

This example combines all four binding types:

```text
[(ngModel)]        → two-way binding
{{ value }}        → interpolation
[disabled]="..."   → property binding
(click)="..."      → event binding
```

---

# 12. Comparison of all four bindings

| Binding | Syntax | Direction | Main purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| Interpolation | `{{ value }}` | Component → template | Display text |
| Property binding | `[property]="value"` | Component → element | Set element properties |
| Event binding | `(event)="method()"` | Template → component | Respond to user actions |
| Two-way binding | `[(ngModel)]="value"` | Both directions | Keep data and input synchronized |

---

# Quick revision

Two-way binding uses:

```html
[(ngModel)]="property"
```

It keeps a component property and a form control synchronized.

Remember:

- Import `FormsModule`.
- Add `FormsModule` to the component's `imports` array.
- Use `[(ngModel)]` for inputs, dropdowns, and checkboxes.
- Text inputs usually use string properties.
- Checkboxes usually use boolean properties.
- Use `name` attributes when controls are inside a form.
- Two-way binding combines property binding and event binding.

## One-line takeaway

> Two-way binding uses `[(ngModel)]` to keep the component data and the form control synchronized in both directions.
